to some breaking news now and an indian  court has upheld a ban preventing women  wearing headscarves in the classrooms in  the state of karnataka elizabeth peranam  is live for us in new delhi hello there  elizabeth what happened in court  hello emily well the  full bench of the karnataka high court  is still  reading its judgment but what it said so  far is that it's dismissing  various pleas by students in karnataka  who were challenging  the ban on the wearing of headscarves  and the high court went on to say that  headscarves are not  part of an essential practice in the  religion of islam and because they're  not part of an essential practice the  wearing of headscarves is not protected  under article 25 of the constitution  which gives everyone in india the right  to religious freedom now that's what we  know so far the students had been  arguing in the karnataka high court that  there is no law which prohibits them  from wearing the headscarf and also that  the constitution allows them to while  the karnataka state government was  saying that  while they agree that there is no law  that schools and colleges are allowed to  make up their own rules about the  wearing of about displaying religious  symbols now this is something that this  issue erupted earlier on this year in  udupi when  it was just one school the principal of  one school who asked students wearing  headscarves to remove them it spread to  other schools and colleges in udupi and  then in other parts of bangalore leading  to protests in support of the students  wearing headscarves and also protests  against them by hindu nationalists this  is why there is now a all schools and  colleges are closed in udupi district  today there are there is also a ban on  people gathering in a number of  districts in the state of karnataka  including the capital bengaluru another  city  mangaluru for a week with government  officials asking people not to  protest this decision but also not to  celebrate it and that is because it is  an issue that  has  polarized many people not just in  karnataka but beyond two  and elizabeth what's likely to happen  going forward are there any other  avenues of appeal or are people likely  to react  well we'll have to see what impact this  decision by the karnataka karnataka high  court has  but as i mentioned it has really  polarized people there were many  protests in karnataka in fact all  educational institutions in the state  were closed for at least a week because  of protests that were sometimes turning  turning violent and it is  you can say for certain that this is not  going to be the end of this issue  because the karnataka high court is the  highest court in the state of karnataka  but this is very likely to go to india's  top court to  the supreme court where the judges there  will have to make a decision and again  weigh up whether the indian constitution  which gives everyone the right to  religious freedom whether it will also  allow  students muslim students to wear  headscarves for now  the karnataka high court has held up the  ban saying that they're not allowed to  interestingly it also said that  students won't be allowed to wear  saffron scarves which is something that  we saw by hindu students who were  protesting against muslim students  wearing headscarves  certainly not going to be the end of  this issue thank you very much for that  update elizabeth paranam live for us in  new delhi
 

